The Semantic Integration of Evil (SIE) Framework


Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI: chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Gemini 

Abstract

Human minds do not merely detect harmful events. They compress heterogeneous harms into moral categories, attribute those categories to agents, and — under identifiable conditions — transform those categories into personified, symbolically transmitted, and cosmologically embedded representations of evil. This paper proposes the Semantic Integration of Evil (SIE) framework: a recursive, generative account of how moral abstraction, agent attribution, personification, symbolization, and cultural transmission form a self-stabilizing loop.

SIE is not a claim to have discovered any single mechanism. Existing literatures already document the components: person-centered moral judgment, dyadic completion, moral typecasting, the myth of pure evil, demonization, moral disengagement, moral exclusion, dehumanization, moralization, minimally counterintuitive concepts, cultural evolution of moralizing gods, belief in pure evil, and the cognition of severe moral failure. The claim of SIE is architectural. It holds that these mechanisms do not merely coexist; they compose into a recurrent pipeline with feedback, such that an established personified evil category changes the interpretation of new ambiguous harms, strengthens agent attribution, and increases the cultural stability of the category itself.

The central theoretical construct is the compression–prediction loop:

\text{Harm} \rightarrow \text{Moralization} \rightarrow \text{Agent Attribution} \rightarrow \text{Personification} \rightarrow \text{Symbolization} \rightarrow \text{Cultural Memory} \rightarrow \text{Prior} \rightarrow \text{Prediction} \rightarrow \text{New Moralization}

This loop makes moral categories function not only as summaries of the past but as generative models of the future. The paper formalizes the resulting trade-off using a rate–distortion–perception framework: stronger personified evil models can classify extreme moral events more efficiently, but they also increase false-positive evil attribution under ambiguity.

The revised version incorporates verified citation corrections, adopts the Belief in Pure Evil / Belief in Pure Good scales as a core measurement instrument, replaces contested hypersensitive agency detection with threat-mediated illusory pattern perception, and specifies a dynamic recursive model rather than a static logistic snapshot. SIE specifies eight lethal tests under which it should be abandoned, a cross-cultural coding scheme designed to distinguish branching pathways, and an adversarial LLM protocol whose purpose is to determine whether SIE deserves to exist.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 The Problem

Human societies routinely compress heterogeneous harmful events — murder, betrayal, torture, exploitation, desecration, collective violence — into shared moral categories such as wrong, wicked, cruel, evil, monstrous. These events are causally and phenomenologically dissimilar. Yet human minds and cultures treat them as members of a common class.

Two questions follow.

First, how does a human mind transform multiple distinct harms into one abstract moral category?

Second, why do some of those abstract categories acquire agency, a name, a face, and eventually a place in cosmology?

These questions already have substantial answers in the existing literature. An honest research program must name those answers at the outset.

1.2 Existing Mechanisms Already Cover Much of the Pipeline

Several research programs already model parts of the proposed trajectory.

Moral categorization and person-centered judgment.
Moral psychology has increasingly shown that moral judgment targets persons rather than isolated acts. Observers infer stable moral character from behavior, and moral categories function as psychological kinds. This supplies a mechanism for moving from “this act is wrong” to “this person is bad.”

Dyadic morality and dyadic completion.
Gray and colleagues propose that moral cognition is organized around a dyadic template of an intentional agent and a suffering patient. Dyadic completion is the tendency to see blameworthy agents in response to suffering patients, and to see suffering patients in response to morally bad agents. This mechanism has been used to explain belief in God, moral typecasting, and the special sting of malicious harm.

Moral typecasting.
People who are cast as agents of evil are perceived as less vulnerable to being harmed, while victims are perceived as less capable of harmful action. This shows that moral categories already contain implicit role structures.

The myth of pure evil.
Baumeister proposes that ordinary people hold a folk schema of evil as deliberate, sadistic, essentially different from ordinary selves, and located outside the in-group. Crucially, Baumeister finds that perpetrators of violence often understand themselves as victims defending against evil. “Evil” is thus often a label applied to outgroups rather than a stable property of actors.

Demonization.
Van Prooijen and van de Veer (2010) treat demonizing as a specific form of impression formation in which perpetrators are understood through anthropomorphized cultural symbols of evil, such as demons or the devil. Their work shows that demonization is amplified by cognitive load and prototypical evilness.

The cognition of severe moral failure.
Govrin (2018) proposes a prototype model of perceived evil, in which extreme moral violations are judged against a structured prototype. The model already includes feedback loops and top-down influences on evil perception. SIE does not compete with Govrin’s mechanism; it asks what happens when Govrin’s prototype is recursively stabilized at the cultural level.

Moral disengagement and moral exclusion.
Bandura specifies how vilification, dehumanization, and blame of victims disengage moral self-sanctions. Opotow shows that a shrinking scope of justice narrows who is entitled to moral consideration. Haslam’s taxonomy explicitly includes demonization as a node within dehumanization.

Moralization.
Rozin provides a general account of how preferences become moral values, both in individuals and cultures.

Religious transmission and moralizing gods.
Boyer shows that minimally counterintuitive concepts are more memorable and transmissible than fully intuitive or maximally counterintuitive ones. Norenzayan extends this into an account of why moralizing supernatural agents spread in large anonymous societies.

Belief in pure evil as an individual-difference construct.
Webster and Saucier (2013) developed and validated separate scales for Belief in Pure Evil (BPE) and Belief in Pure Good (BPG). BPE is a reliable, unitary, temporally stable construct that predicts harsher punishment, demonization, and moral condemnation. It operationalizes precisely the personification tendency SIE requires at the individual level.

Threat, illusory pattern perception, and agency detection.
Van Prooijen, Douglas, and De Inocencio (2018) show that existential threat increases beliefs in conspiracies and the supernatural through hypersensitive agency detection and illusory pattern perception. This provides a current, empirically supported alternative to older, contested accounts of agency detection.

The historical case of Satan.
Pagels documents that the Hebrew Bible’s ha-satan is an adversary or divine prosecutor, not a cosmic enemy. The New Testament transforms this figure into a cosmic opponent associated with evil. Pagels reads this transformation as a social device through which early Christian communities cast internal opponents — Jews, pagans, heretics — as cosmic villains.

Given this prior art, the question is no longer whether humans moralize harm, attribute evil to agents, personify evil, or use evil categories for social exclusion. Those phenomena are established.

The remaining question is architectural.

1.3 The SIE Claim

The Semantic Integration of Evil hypothesis makes three claims.

C1 — Cognitive architecture.
Humans are biased toward composing the known mechanisms into a recurrent loop when processing extreme moral violations. The loop is more likely to run to completion when harm is high, intentionality is high, agency is uncertain, and threat is salient. The bias is real but not inevitable: it is a conditional bias, not a universal output.

C2 — Cultural evolution.
Communities that compress diverse harms into personified moral categories gain coordination, transmission, and boundary-enforcement advantages under certain conditions. These categories are more memorable, more communicable, and more useful for moral triage than unintegrated lists of harms.

C3 — Historical contingency.
The specific trajectory from bureaucratic adversary to cosmic evil occurs under intergroup threat, contested authority, and dualist cultural contact. It is not a universal invariant.

SIE’s contribution is not any single mechanism. It is the claim that these mechanisms compose into a self-reinforcing generative system, and that this composition has measurable consequences above and beyond the sum of its parts.

