2. Definitions and Conceptual Separation

SIE must separate phenomena that are often conflated.

2.1 Three Distinct Transitions

The theory distinguishes:

A1 — Moral abstraction.
An action or event is classified as morally wrong.

\text{“This action is evil.”}

A2 — Agent attribution.
A specific agent is identified as the source of the morally wrong action.

\text{“Someone evil did this.”}

A3 — Personification.
The abstract moral category itself becomes an agent.

\text{“Evil itself is an agent.”}

These are not the same. A person may say “murder is evil” without saying “an evil person did this.” A person may say “an evil person did this” without personifying evil as a cosmic force. Personification is the further step by which the category acquires agency independently of any specific human actor.

Operational distinction.
SIE uses the following operational criteria:

· A2 is present when participants or texts attribute blame to a specific human or non-human actor.
· A3 is present when the abstract moral category is treated as having intentions, desires, plans, or causal powers in its own right, independent of any named human perpetrator.

This distinction is not merely semantic. It isolates the specific addition that cultural personification makes beyond ordinary agent attribution.

2.2 Extended Stages

For the full pipeline, SIE defines:

· A_1: moral abstraction
· A_2: agent attribution
· A_3: personification
· A_4: symbolic stabilization
· A_5: cosmological integration

Cultures may progress through some, all, or none of these stages. Some may show A_1 \rightarrow A_2 but never A_3. Others may reach A_3 but not A_5. The theory does not require a universal trajectory.

2.3 Recursive Architecture

The central construct is not a linear chain. It is a loop:

\boxed{
\text{Experience} \rightarrow \text{Abstraction} \rightarrow \text{Personification} \rightarrow \text{Symbolization} \rightarrow \text{Cultural Transmission} \rightarrow \text{Prior Beliefs} \rightarrow \text{Interpretation of New Experience}
}

Once a culture has established a personified evil category, that category becomes a prior for interpreting new events. The concept of evil no longer merely summarizes the past. It becomes a generative model of the future.

This recursive property is the heart of SIE.

