3. The Recursive Generative Architecture

3.1 From Chain to Loop

A simple pipeline would be:

\text{Harm} \rightarrow \text{Moralization} \rightarrow \text{Personification} \rightarrow \text{Symbol}

But this is incomplete. It treats the process as ending at symbolization. In reality, symbols feed back into perception.

The full loop is:

\boxed{
\text{Harm} \rightarrow \text{Moralization} \rightarrow \text{Personification} \rightarrow \text{Symbol} \rightarrow \text{Cultural Memory} \rightarrow \text{Prior} \rightarrow \text{Prediction} \rightarrow \text{New Moralization}
}

This loop makes the system self-stabilizing. Once a personified evil category is culturally available, it increases the probability that ambiguous harms are classified as evil, attributed to agents, and assimilated into the existing symbolic framework.

3.2 Generative Model

Let M be a culturally transmitted moral model. The model takes an observed event x and produces a prediction about its moral status:

\hat{y} = M(x)

where \hat{y} is the predicted moral category, such as “evil” or “not evil.”

The model is learned from past events:

M = \mathcal{L}(E_{\text{past}})

But once learned, it is applied to new events:

\hat{y}_{n+1} = M(x_{n+1})

Thus the concept of evil is not merely a compressed description of past harms. It is a predictive model for interpreting future harms.

3.3 The Loop Changes Perception

The loop generates a specific causal prediction:

Cultural availability of a personified evil category increases the probability that ambiguous harmful events will be interpreted through that category.

Formally:

P(\text{evil classification} \mid \text{ambiguous event}) = f(H, I, U, T, M_{\text{prior}})

where H is harm, I is intentionality, U is uncertainty, T is threat, and M_{\text{prior}} is the culturally available moral model.

The prior M_{\text{prior}} is not merely an additive factor. It interacts with uncertainty and threat. When an event is ambiguous and threatening, the prior should exert its strongest influence.

3.4 Dynamic Recursive Model

To capture feedback, SIE specifies a dynamic state-space model rather than a static regression.

Let M_t be the strength of a personified evil model in a community at time t. Let x_t be the event observed at time t, with features H_t, I_t, U_t, T_t.

The probability that the event is interpreted as evil and assimilated into the personified category is:

P(E_t = 1 \mid x_t, M_t) = \sigma\!\big(\beta_0 + \beta_H H_t + \beta_I I_t + \beta_U U_t + \beta_T T_t + \beta_M M_t + \beta_{MU} M_t U_t + \beta_{MT} M_t T_t\big)

where \sigma is the logistic function.

If the event is interpreted through the personified category, the cultural model updates:

M_{t+1} = (1-\eta)M_t + \eta \cdot S_t

where \eta is the learning rate and S_t is the strength of the personified interpretation generated by the event. S_t can be modeled as:

S_t = \sigma\!\big(\alpha + \gamma P(E_t=1 \mid x_t, M_t) + \epsilon_t\big)

This creates a recurrent system. When \beta_M > 0, higher M_t increases the probability of personified interpretation, which in turn increases M_{t+1}. The loop is self-reinforcing but not necessarily explosive, because the logistic function bounds probabilities and the learning rate controls the speed of cultural change.

This dynamic model generates the testable prediction of recursive stabilization:

M_t \text{ predicts } M_{t+1}

even after controlling for the occurrence of new harmful events.

