4. Semantic Compression as Generative Compression

4.1 The Core Trade-Off

Moral abstraction functions as a form of cognitive-cultural compression.

A culture observes many distinct harmful events:

E = \{e_1, e_2, \ldots, e_n\}

It constructs a category:

M = \text{EVIL}

This category compresses the events. But the category is then used to interpret new events:

M \rightarrow \hat{e}_{n+1}

where \hat{e}_{n+1} is the predicted interpretation of a new event from the preexisting concept.

The loop is therefore:

\boxed{
\text{Experience} \rightarrow \text{Compression} \rightarrow \text{Model} \rightarrow \text{Prediction}
}

4.2 Rate–Distortion–Perception Formalization

SIE formalizes the compression–prediction trade-off using the rate–distortion–perception framework from information theory, following Sims (2016).

Let:

· D(E, M) be the distortion or loss of detail when events are compressed into category M.
· C(M) be the complexity of the representation.
· F(M) be the false-positive rate when M is applied to ambiguous new events.

The compression objective is:

\mathcal{L}(M) = D(E, M) + \lambda C(M)

But this is incomplete. The predictive use of M introduces a second cost:

\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}(M) = D(E, M) + \lambda C(M) + \mu F(M)

where \mu controls the cost of false-positive evil attributions.

The central SIE prediction is:

As C(M) decreases — for example, when evil is personified into a single agent — compression efficiency increases, but the false-positive rate F(M) also increases under ambiguity.

There is a structural trade-off:

\text{Compression efficiency} \leftrightarrow \text{Representational distortion}

Personified evil categories are highly compressed and highly generative. They are efficient for moral triage, but they are also more likely to produce false positives when events are ambiguous.

4.3 Operationalizing the Trade-Off

To make the trade-off falsifiable, SIE requires independent measurement of D, C, and F.

· D(E, M) is measured as the amount of event detail lost when participants recall or reconstruct a set of harm events under a personified versus non-personified moral model.
· C(M) is measured as the number of distinct categories, entities, or inferential steps required to represent the moral model.
· F(M) is measured behaviorally as the proportion of ambiguous events incorrectly classified as evil, or the proportion of neutral agents incorrectly attributed evil intent.

A false positive is not merely a strong moral judgment. It is an evil attribution that would not have been made under a non-personified moral model with access to the same event features. This definition prevents circularity.

4.4 Personification Reduces Complexity

Personification is a radical form of compression.

Instead of maintaining a distribution of many harmful acts, a culture can encode evil as a single agent with predictable intentions:

M_{\text{personified}} = \text{“Evil wants, plans, tempts, attacks.”}

This representation is compact, memorable, and communicable. It can be invoked across many contexts. But it also distorts causal attribution. Events without human perpetrators may be falsely attributed to the personified agent. Events caused by multiple interacting factors may be reduced to a single malevolent will.

