5. Computational Model

SIE includes a computational scaffold designed to generate testable predictions.

5.1 Event Representation

Let each observed event be:

x = (H, I, A, V, T, U)

where:

· H = perceived harm
· I = inferred intentionality
· A = inferred agency
· V = perceived moral violation
· T = perceived threat
· U = explanatory uncertainty

5.2 Moral Abstraction

The probability of classifying an event as morally wrong is:

P(A_1 \mid x) = \sigma(\beta_0 + \beta_H H + \beta_I I + \beta_V V + \beta_{HI} HI + \beta_{HV} HV + \epsilon)

Key interactions:

· H \times I: harm without intentionality is less likely to be moralized as evil.
· H \times V: perceived violation may amplify the effect of harm.

5.3 Agent Attribution

The probability of attributing the event to a specific evil agent is:

P(A_2 \mid x, A_1) = \sigma(\gamma_0 + \gamma_A A + \gamma_I I + \gamma_T T + \gamma_{AT} A T + \gamma_{PA} P A + \epsilon)

where P is illusory pattern perception, following van Prooijen, Douglas, and De Inocencio (2018).

Agency and threat should interact: under threat, observers are more likely to seek identifiable agents. Illusory pattern perception mediates the effect of threat on agency attribution. This replaces older hypersensitive agency detection accounts, which have been criticized for lacking direct empirical support.

5.4 Personification

The probability of personifying the abstract moral category itself is:

P(A_3 \mid x, A_1, A_2, M_{\text{prior}}, BPE) = \sigma(\alpha_0 + \alpha_E E(x) + \alpha_U U + \alpha_T T + \alpha_C C + \alpha_{EU} EU + \alpha_{TC} TC + \alpha_M M_{\text{prior}} + \alpha_B BPE + \epsilon)

where:

· E(x) = strength of perceived evil from the moral abstraction model.
· U = explanatory uncertainty.
· T = threat.
· C = availability of alternative causal explanations.
· M_{\text{prior}} = cultural availability of personified evil categories.
· BPE = individual Belief in Pure Evil, measured with Webster and Saucier (2013).
· E \times U = interaction between evil strength and uncertainty.
· T \times C = interaction between threat and alternative explanations.
· M_{\text{prior}} is the prior from the cultural loop.

The inclusion of BPE makes individual differences explicit. SIE predicts that the effect of M_{\text{prior}} on personification is stronger for individuals high in BPE.

5.5 Cultural Updating

Once personification occurs, the cultural model is updated:

M_{t+1} = (1 - \eta) M_t + \eta S_t

where S_t is the new symbolic representation, and \eta is the learning rate.

If M_t is already strong, new ambiguous events are more likely to be assimilated into the existing model, increasing M_{t+1} further.

This is the self-reinforcing loop.

5.6 Multilevel Structure

The model is multilevel.

Level 1 — Event features: harm, intentionality, agency, violation, threat, uncertainty.
Level 2 — Individual differences: Belief in Pure Evil / Belief in Pure Good, agency detection, moralization tendency, need for closure, religiosity.
Level 3 — Cultural availability: presence of personified evil concepts, dualist cosmology, out-group threat.
Level 4 — Historical context: intergroup conflict, institutional stability, cultural contact.

Random slopes are expected. For example, the effect of uncertainty on personification should be stronger in cultures with high personified evil availability.

