6. Core Predictions

SIE generates the following falsifiable predictions.

P1 — Harm–intentionality interaction.
Higher harm increases moral abstraction, but the effect is conditional on intentionality. Accidental harm produces weaker moralization.

P2 — Agency under uncertainty.
When a harmful event is difficult to explain, observers are more likely to seek an agent, especially when cultural personified evil concepts are available.

P3 — Threat amplification.
Collective threat increases the probability that evil is attributed to identifiable agents or groups. This effect is stronger under existential threat and is mediated by illusory pattern perception.

P4 — Cultural availability effect.
Individuals exposed to culturally available personifications of evil use those representations more readily when interpreting ambiguous harmful events. This effect is stronger for individuals high in Belief in Pure Evil.

P5 — Feedback loop.
Once an evil category is culturally established, it influences the interpretation of subsequent ambiguous behavior, increasing false-positive evil attributions.

P6 — Personification as compression.
Cultures with personified evil categories show better memory and communication of multiple harm events but higher false-positive rates for evil attribution in ambiguous events. This trade-off follows the rate–distortion–perception structure.

P7 — Social polarization.
When an evil representation becomes associated with an out-group, moral condemnation becomes more resistant to counter-evidence, beyond ordinary negative evaluation.

P8 — Dehumanization pathway.
Strong enemy–evil associations predict greater dehumanization after controlling for general out-group hostility.

P9 — Branching pathways.
Cultures may show A_1 \rightarrow A_2 without A_3, or A_1 \rightarrow A_2 \rightarrow A_3 without A_5. The distribution of pathways should not be random but patterned by threat, uncertainty, and intergroup conflict.

P10 — Recursive stabilization.
The strength of a personified evil model at time t predicts the strength of the same model at time t+1, even after controlling for the occurrence of new harmful events.

P11 — Compression–distortion trade-off.
As the complexity of the moral model decreases, compression efficiency increases, but false-positive evil attribution under ambiguity also increases.

P12 — Moderators of destructive pathway.
The destructive pathway is strengthened by existential threat and identity fusion, and weakened by institutional checks and intergroup contact.

P13 — Individual difference moderation.
The effect of cultural personified evil availability on evil classification is moderated by Belief in Pure Evil. High-BPE individuals show a stronger cultural availability effect than low-BPE individuals.

