7. Lethal Tests

SIE should be abandoned or substantially revised if any of the following conditions holds.

L1 — No composition effect.
If the pipeline levels do not predict one another better than their independent base rates, SIE is unnecessary.

L2 — Dyadic completion plus moralization suffices.
If personification of evil is fully explained by dyadic completion and moralization, even when a human perpetrator has already been identified, then SIE adds nothing. The test is differential: SIE must predict personification above and beyond dyadic completion after controlling for known harm and intentionality features.

L3 — Myth of pure evil plus cultural transmission suffices.
If Baumeister’s folk schema plus standard cultural transmission models explains all personification and demonization effects after controlling for threat and out-group status, then SIE is redundant. SIE must show that the recursive feedback loop improves prediction beyond a one-pass schema model.

L4 — Govrin’s prototype model with feedback suffices.
If Govrin’s (2018) prototype model of evil perception, which already includes top-down influences and feedback loops, outperforms SIE’s full recursive cultural model across cultures and over time, then SIE should be abandoned. SIE’s differential claim is cultural-level stabilization and compression–distortion trade-off, not individual-level perception alone.

L5 — No feedback loop.
If culturally available personified evil categories do not alter the interpretation of new ambiguous events over time, the recursive architecture is false.

L6 — No compression trade-off.
If personified evil categories do not produce higher false-positive rates under ambiguity relative to non-personified moral categories, the compression–distortion trade-off is unsupported.

L7 — No cross-cultural recurrence beyond diffusion.
If the structural co-occurrence of moral abstraction, agent attribution, personification, and cosmic integration does not recur independently across separated cultures beyond diffusion, the cognitive bias claim is weakened. This test is strong only when phylogenetic or network controls are used; absence of recurrence after such controls is evidence against SIE.

L8 — No branching pattern.
If all cultures either show the full pipeline or none of it, with no intermediate pathways, the proposed stage decomposition is wrong.

L9 — Belief in Pure Evil fully mediates all SIE effects.
If individual differences in Belief in Pure Evil account for all cultural-level effects, then SIE’s cultural architecture is unnecessary. SIE must show that cultural availability adds predictive power beyond individual BPE.

