9. Experimental Paradigms

9.1 Factorial Vignette Study

A preliminary experiment could use a factorial design:

· H = harm (high/low)
· I = intentionality (high/low)
· U = uncertainty (high/low)
· T = threat (high/low)
· M_{\text{prior}} = cultural availability of personified evil (primed/not primed)

Primary individual-difference measure: Webster and Saucier (2013) Belief in Pure Evil / Belief in Pure Good scales.

Dependent measures:

· moral wrongness
· perceived evil
· perceived intentionality
· agency attribution
· personification tendency
· supernatural attribution
· dehumanization
· false-positive evil classification
· confidence in moral judgment
· punishment preference

SIE predicts that personified evil priming increases evil attribution to ambiguous harmful events, especially for high-BPE participants.

9.2 Compression–Distortion Task

Participants learn a moral category from a set of harmful events. Then they classify new ambiguous events.

The prediction is that participants trained with a personified evil category will:

· classify morally extreme events more efficiently
· show higher false-positive evil classification for ambiguous events
· show stronger resistance to counter-evidence

This task directly operationalizes D, C, and F from Section 4.

9.3 Cultural Priming

Participants are primed with personified evil symbols or neutral symbols. They then evaluate ambiguous harmful events involving in-group and out-group members.

The prediction is that personified evil priming increases evil attribution to out-group members and increases dehumanization, beyond general negative affect.

9.4 Transmission Chain Experiment

Participants are trained with either a personified evil category or a distributed list of harm types. They then transmit the representation to a second participant, who transmits to a third, and so on.

SIE predicts that personified models survive transmission with higher fidelity but produce more false positives when later used to classify ambiguous events.

9.5 Longitudinal Cultural Analysis

Using historical corpora, measure the strength of personified evil categories over time. Test whether strength at time t predicts strength at time t+1, controlling for the occurrence of harmful events.

This directly tests the recursive stabilization prediction and the dynamic model in Section 3.4.

