10. Historical Case Studies

Satan is one case study among several.

10.1 Satan

In early Hebrew contexts, ha-satan is an adversary or divine prosecutor. In later New Testament and apocalyptic traditions, Satan becomes a cosmic opponent. Pagels reads this as a social device for casting internal opponents as cosmic villains.

SIE predicts that this transformation occurred under intergroup threat, contested authority, and dualist cultural contact.

10.2 Angra Mainyu

Zoroastrianism develops a cosmic opponent within a dualist framework. This is a case where personification and cosmological integration occur together.

10.3 Māra

Buddhism personifies delusion, death, and sensual temptation as Māra. This is personification without a fully dualist cosmic enemy.

10.4 Iblis

Islam has a specific fallen being, Iblis, but not always a fully dualist cosmic principle. This shows personification without full cosmological integration.

10.5 Secular Demonization

Modern political ideologies demonize enemies without supernatural commitment. Lester Kurtz’s case study of the Gulf conflict and the demonization of Saddam Hussein provides a detailed example of how a political leader can be transformed into a personified embodiment of evil without a supernatural cosmology. This shows that the pipeline can produce enemy construction even when cosmological integration is absent.

The comparative goal is to identify whether the same structural conditions produce personified evil representations across different cultural vocabularies.

