11. Evolutionary Account

SIE does not claim that Homo sapiens evolved specifically to believe in evil.

A more conservative hypothesis is:

\text{Biological evolution} + \text{Cultural evolution} \rightarrow \text{Semantic architectures of evil}

Humans evolved general capacities for threat detection, social learning, agency attribution, norm enforcement, coalition management, memory, narrative reasoning, and moral evaluation. Cultural evolution subsequently combined these capacities into increasingly complex representations of evil.

This is a cultural by-product account. It does not require a dedicated “evil module.”

The evolutionary claim is about cultural attractors and cognitive biases, not about a special adaptation for supernatural evil.

