13. Conclusion

This paper proposes the Semantic Integration of Evil as a recursive, generative framework. It claims that moral abstraction, agent attribution, personification, symbolization, and cultural transmission form a self-stabilizing loop. Once a personified evil category is culturally established, it changes the interpretation of new ambiguous harms, strengthens agent attribution, and stabilizes the category over time.

The theory does not claim that humans universally conceptualize evil in the same way. It does not claim that Satan is the origin of evil. It does not claim that supernatural representations are irrational. It does not claim that personification necessarily produces violence.

It claims something more precise and more testable:

\boxed{
\text{Harm} \rightarrow \text{Moralization} \rightarrow \text{Personification} \rightarrow \text{Symbol} \rightarrow \text{Cultural Memory} \rightarrow \text{Prior} \rightarrow \text{Prediction} \rightarrow \text{New Moralization}
}

may be a recurring property of human cognition and cultural evolution.

The central scientific trade-off is:

\text{Compression efficiency} \leftrightarrow \text{Representational distortion}

Personified evil categories are powerful generative models. They are efficient for moral triage, communication, and coordination. But they also increase the risk of false-positive evil attribution under ambiguity.

The ultimate objective is not to prove the theory. It is to determine whether the theory deserves to exist at all.

If existing cognitive science can explain the entire phenomenon without SIE, the construct should be abandoned. If, however, evidence reveals a recurring recursive architecture — one that connects moral abstraction, agent attribution, personification, symbolic compression, social identity, and cultural transmission — then SIE could provide a bridge between research traditions that are usually studied separately.

In that case, the study of “evil” would become something more fundamental: a case study in how Homo sapiens converts experience into meaning, meaning into symbols, and symbols into civilization.

