Appendix A: Full Formal Model Specification

A.1 Event Representation

Each observed event is encoded as:

x = (H, I, A, V, T, U)

where:

· H = perceived harm: rated 1–7
· I = inferred intentionality: rated 1–7
· A = inferred agency: rated 1–7
· V = perceived moral violation: rated 1–7
· T = perceived threat: rated 1–7
· U = explanatory uncertainty: rated 1–7

Individual-level moderators:

· BPE = Belief in Pure Evil score, standardized
· P = illusory pattern perception propensity, standardized
· NFC = need for closure, optional

Cultural-level moderator:

· M_t = strength of culturally available personified evil model at time t, range 0–1

---

A.2 Stage 1: Moral Abstraction

P(A_1 \mid x) =
\sigma\!\left(
\beta_0 + \beta_H H + \beta_I I + \beta_V V + \beta_{HI} HI + \beta_{HV} HV
\right)

Predicted signs:

· \beta_H > 0
· \beta_I > 0
· \beta_V > 0
· \beta_{HI} > 0: harm is moralized more when intentional
· \beta_{HV} > 0: harm is moralized more when perceived as violation

---

A.3 Stage 2: Agent Attribution

P(A_2 \mid x, A_1) =
\sigma\!\left(
\gamma_0 + \gamma_A A + \gamma_I I + \gamma_T T + \gamma_{AT} A T + \gamma_{PA} P A + \gamma_{PT} P T
\right)

Predicted signs:

· \gamma_A > 0
· \gamma_T > 0
· \gamma_{AT} > 0: threat amplifies agency seeking
· \gamma_{PA} > 0: pattern perception enhances agency attribution
· \gamma_{PT} > 0: threat and pattern perception jointly increase agent attribution

Threat–pattern perception mediation:

P = \alpha_0 + \alpha_T T + \varepsilon

A_2^* = \gamma_0 + \gamma_A A + \gamma_P P + \gamma_{PA} PA

This follows van Prooijen, Douglas, and De Inocencio (2018), in which threat increases illusory pattern perception, which in turn predicts supernatural and conspiratorial attributions.

---

A.4 Stage 3: Personification

P(A_3 \mid x, A_1, A_2, M_t, BPE) =
\sigma\!\left(
\alpha_0 + \alpha_E E(x) + \alpha_U U + \alpha_T T + \alpha_C C + \alpha_{EU} EU + \alpha_{TC} TC + \alpha_M M_t + \alpha_B BPE + \alpha_{MB} M_t BPE
\right)

where:

· E(x) = predicted strength of perceived evil from Stage 1: E(x) = P(A_1 \mid x)
· C = availability of alternative causal explanations, rated 1–7
· M_t = cultural availability of personified evil model
· BPE = individual Belief in Pure Evil score

Predicted signs:

· \alpha_U > 0
· \alpha_T > 0
· \alpha_C < 0
· \alpha_{EU} > 0: uncertainty amplifies effect of perceived evil
· \alpha_{TC} < 0: threat and alternative explanations interact negatively
· \alpha_M > 0
· \alpha_B > 0
· \alpha_{MB} > 0: cultural availability effect stronger for high-BPE individuals

---

A.5 Stage 4: Symbolic Stabilization

Symbolization occurs when personification is linguistically or ritually encoded.

P(A_4 \mid A_3, M_t) =
\sigma\!\left(
\phi_0 + \phi_3 A_3 + \phi_M M_t + \phi_{3M} A_3 M_t
\right)

Predicted signs:

· \phi_3 > 0
· \phi_M > 0
· \phi_{3M} > 0: personification more likely to stabilize symbolically when a prior personified model already exists

---

A.6 Stage 5: Cosmological Integration

P(A_5 \mid A_4, T_{\text{exist}}, D) =
\sigma\!\left(
\psi_0 + \psi_4 A_4 + \psi_T T_{\text{exist}} + \psi_D D + \psi_{4T} A_4 T_{\text{exist}}
\right)

where:

· T_{\text{exist}} = existential threat level, historical/collective
· D = dualist cultural contact, 0/1 or continuous

Predicted signs:

· \psi_T > 0
· \psi_D > 0
· \psi_{4T} > 0: symbolic evil more likely to become cosmological under existential threat

---

A.7 Cultural Updating and Recursive Loop

The cultural model updates through time:

M_{t+1} = (1 - \eta) M_t + \eta S_t

where \eta \in [0,1] is the cultural learning rate.

S_t is the strength of personified interpretation generated by events at time t:

S_t = \sigma\!\left(
\alpha + \gamma \, P(A_3 \mid x_t, A_1, A_2, M_t, BPE) + \epsilon_t
\right)

The full recurrence is:

M_{t+1} =
(1 - \eta) M_t +
\eta \,
\sigma\!\left(
\alpha + \gamma \,
\sigma\!\left(
\alpha_0 + \alpha_E E(x_t) + \alpha_U U_t + \alpha_T T_t + \alpha_C C_t + \alpha_{EU} E(x_t)U_t + \alpha_{TC} T_t C_t + \alpha_M M_t + \alpha_B BPE + \alpha_{MB} M_t BPE
\right)
+ \epsilon_t
\right)

This equation defines a nonlinear difference equation in M_t. Under appropriate parameters, it produces self-stabilization, threshold effects, and path dependence.

---

A.8 False-Positive Rate

Define the false-positive evil classification rate for a given cultural model M as:

F(M) =
\frac{1}{N_{\text{amb}}}
\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\text{amb}}}
P(A_3 \mid x_{\text{amb}, i}, M)

where x_{\text{amb}} are ambiguous harmful events with no clear perpetrator.

The SIE prediction is:

\frac{\partial F(M)}{\partial C(M)} < 0

when C(M) is the complexity of the moral representation. In other words, as complexity decreases due to personification, false-positive evil classification increases.

---

A.9 Rate–Distortion–Perception Objective

The cultural moral model M is evaluated by:

\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}(M) =
D(E, M) + \lambda C(M) + \mu F(M)

where:

· D(E, M) = distortion: how much event-specific detail is lost
· C(M) = representation complexity
· F(M) = false-positive rate under ambiguity
· \lambda = cost of complexity
· \mu = cost of false positives

Personified evil models have low C(M), moderate D(E,M), and high F(M). Distributed harm lists have high C(M), low D(E,M), and low F(M). The optimal representation depends on \lambda and \mu.

---

A.10 Multilevel Statistical Model

The full model is multilevel.

Level 1 — Event:

\log\!\left(\frac{P(A_1)}{1-P(A_1)}\right) =
\beta_{0j} + \beta_{1j} H + \beta_{2j} I + \beta_{3j} V + \beta_{4j} HI + \beta_{5j} HV

Level 2 — Individual:

\beta_{0j} = \beta_{00} + u_{0j}

\beta_{kj} = \beta_{k0} + \beta_{kB} BPE_j + u_{kj}

Level 3 — Culture:

\beta_{00} = \beta_{000} + \beta_{00M} M_{\text{culture}} + u_{00k}

Random slopes are modeled for the effects of uncertainty and threat on personification.

