Appendix B: Operationalization and Measurement

B.1 Perceived Harm (H)

Measure: Single item, 1–7 Likert.
Item: “How harmful was the event?”
Anchors: 1 = not at all harmful, 7 = extremely harmful.

---

B.2 Inferred Intentionality (I)

Measure: Three-item composite, \alpha > .85.
Items:

1. “To what extent was the harm intentional?”
2. “To what extent did the actor mean to cause harm?”
3. “To what extent was the outcome deliberate?”

Anchors: 1 = completely accidental, 7 = completely intentional.

---

B.3 Inferred Agency (A)

Measure: Two-item composite.
Items:

1. “To what extent was a specific individual or being responsible for this event?”
2. “To what extent was there an identifiable agent behind this event?”

Anchors: 1 = no identifiable agent, 7 = completely identifiable agent.

---

B.4 Perceived Moral Violation (V)

Measure: Three-item composite.
Items:

1. “This event violated a deeply held moral rule.”
2. “This event is morally wrong.”
3. “This event deserves strong moral condemnation.”

Anchors: 1 = strongly disagree, 7 = strongly agree.

---

B.5 Perceived Threat (T)

Measure: Four-item composite.
Items:

1. “This event makes me feel unsafe.”
2. “This event threatens my community.”
3. “This event could happen again.”
4. “This event threatens the social order.”

Anchors: 1 = strongly disagree, 7 = strongly agree.

---

B.6 Explanatory Uncertainty (U)

Measure: Three-item composite.
Items:

1. “I cannot fully explain why this happened.”
2. “The causes of this event are unclear.”
3. “It is difficult to understand why someone would do this.”

Anchors: 1 = strongly disagree, 7 = strongly agree.

---

B.7 Alternative Causal Explanations (C)

Measure: Three-item composite, reverse-coded.
Items:

1. “This event can be explained by social conditions, mental illness, or other non-moral causes.”
2. “There are many plausible explanations for this event that do not involve evil.”
3. “The causes of this event are complex and multifaceted.”

Anchors: 1 = strongly disagree, 7 = strongly agree. Higher scores indicate greater availability of alternative explanations.

---

B.8 Belief in Pure Evil (BPE)

Instrument: Webster & Saucier (2013) Belief in Pure Evil scale, 22 items.
Scoring: 1–7 Likert, mean composite.
Example items:

· “There are people in this world who are purely evil.”
· “Some people are born evil.”
· “Evil people are completely different from the rest of us.”
· “Some people’s souls are completely corrupt.”

Use: BPE is the primary individual-difference moderator. Standardized scores are entered into all personification and cultural availability models.

---

B.9 Illusory Pattern Perception (P)

Measure: Four-item adaptation from van Prooijen et al. (2018).
Items:

1. “I often see connections between events that others miss.”
2. “Random events often seem to form a meaningful pattern.”
3. “When things go wrong, I look for hidden causes.”
4. “I often notice coincidences that turn out to be meaningful.”

Anchors: 1 = strongly disagree, 7 = strongly agree.

---

B.10 Cultural Availability of Personified Evil (M_{\text{prior}} / M_t)

Measure: Composite index per culture or community.

Components:

1. Presence of a named personified evil entity (0/1)
2. Frequency of personified evil terms in major cultural texts (log-transformed)
3. Existence of rituals or institutions referencing personified evil (0/1)
4. Rated salience of personified evil in public discourse (1–5, averaged across expert coders)

The index is standardized to 0–1.

---

B.11 False-Positive Evil Classification (F)

Operational definition: Proportion of ambiguous vignettes classified as “evil” that a non-personified moral model would classify as “not evil.”

Measurement procedure:

1. Construct ambiguous vignettes with moderate harm, unclear intentionality, and no obvious perpetrator.
2. Obtain baseline classification rates from participants with low BPE and no personified evil prime.
3. False positives are classifications exceeding baseline by more than 2 standard deviations.

---

B.12 Distortion (D)

Measure: Event recall detail loss.

Participants learn a set of 10 harm events. Later, they recall features: perpetrator, cause, context, number of victims, type of harm.

D = 1 - \frac{\text{correctly recalled features}}{\text{total possible features}}

Personified training is predicted to reduce recall of situational detail.

---

B.13 Complexity (C)

Measure: Number of distinct entities, causal nodes, or categories required to represent the moral model.

Operational coding:

· Non-personified model: one category per harm type.
· Personified model: one entity plus minimal causal structure.

C(M) is normalized to 0–1.

