Appendix D: Experimental Protocols and Sample Vignettes

D.1 Factorial Vignette Study

Design: 2 \times 2 \times 2 \times 2 \times 2 between-subjects factorial, with optional personified evil prime.

Factors:

1. Harm: high / low
2. Intentionality: high / low
3. Uncertainty: high / low
4. Threat: high / low
5. Cultural availability prime: personified evil / neutral

Sample vignette — high harm, high intentionality, high uncertainty, high threat, no prime:

A fire destroyed a community center in a small town. Investigators discovered that the fire was set deliberately using an accelerant. The person responsible left a message threatening further attacks. No one has been identified. The town is now on high alert.

Sample vignette — low harm, low intentionality, low uncertainty, low threat:

A small misunderstanding at a workplace led to minor property damage. The incident was clearly accidental, and the person involved immediately apologized and paid for repairs.

Dependent measures:

· Moral wrongness (1–7)
· Perceived evil (1–7)
· Perceived intentionality (1–7)
· Agency attribution (1–7)
· Personification tendency (1–7)
· Supernatural attribution (1–7)
· Dehumanization (1–7)
· False-positive evil classification (binary)
· Confidence in moral judgment (1–7)
· Punishment preference (1–7)

---

D.2 Compression–Distortion Task

Phase 1: Learning

Participants are randomly assigned to one of two training conditions:

1. Personified condition: Learn that extreme harms are caused by “the Evil One,” a single malevolent agent.
2. Distributed condition: Learn a list of ten distinct harm categories, each with its own causes and features.

Phase 2: Recall

Participants recall as many harm types as possible.

Phase 3: Classification

Participants classify 20 new vignettes as evil or not evil. Ten vignettes are extreme, ten are ambiguous.

Predictions:

· Personified condition: higher recall accuracy for extreme harms, higher false-positive classification of ambiguous harms, higher confidence.

---

D.3 Cultural Priming Experiment

Design: 2 (prime: personified evil vs neutral) × 2 (target: ingroup vs outgroup) between-subjects.

Prime stimuli:

· Personified evil prime: brief narrative describing an ancient enemy known as “the Dark One” who corrupts human actions.
· Neutral prime: brief narrative describing geological formations.

Dependent measures:

· Evil attribution
· Dehumanization
· Punishment preference
· Confidence

---

D.4 Transmission Chain Experiment

Procedure:

1. Participant 1 learns a moral representation (personified or distributed).
2. Participant 1 writes a summary.
3. Participant 2 reads Participant 1’s summary and writes their own.
4. Repeat for 5–8 generations.
5. Measure fidelity, compression, and later classification of ambiguous events.

Predictions:

· Personified representations survive with higher fidelity.
· Personified representations produce more false-positive evil classifications after transmission.

---

D.5 Longitudinal Cultural Analysis

Corpus selection:

· Historical texts, newspapers, legal records, religious documents, political speeches.
· Time series spanning at least 50 years.

Coding:

· Strength of personified evil category M_t per year or decade.
· Occurrence of major harmful events.

Statistical model:

M_{t+1} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 M_t + \beta_2 H_t + \varepsilon_t

SIE predicts \beta_1 > 0 after controlling for H_t.

