Appendix F: Adversarial LLM Protocol

F.1 Purpose

To determine whether SIE adds differential predictive value beyond existing theories.

F.2 Procedure

Step 1: Provide each LLM agent with:

· Full SIE framework
· Summaries of 10 competing theories
· Definitions of all SIE constructs
· Lethal tests L1–L9

Step 2: Prompt:

“You are a skeptical cognitive scientist. Identify all reasons why the Semantic Integration of Evil framework should not exist. Compare SIE directly with each of the following theories: dyadic completion, moralization, agency detection, psychological essentialism, myth of pure evil, demonization, dehumanization, moral disengagement, minimally counterintuitive concepts, cultural evolution of moralizing gods, Belief in Pure Evil, Govrin’s prototype model, rate–distortion perception. For each theory, state whether it can explain the same phenomena without SIE. Then state what empirical observation would make SIE unnecessary.”

Step 3: Run with at least three independent LLM agents with different model families.

Step 4: Code responses for:

· Whether SIE is judged redundant
· Which differential predictions are identified
· Which lethal tests are judged sufficient

Step 5: Pre-register decision rule:

· If a majority of independent agents judge SIE redundant and identify no unique differential predictions, abandon or substantially revise.
· If agents identify unique recursive composition, cultural feedback, or compression–distortion trade-off, retain.

